\title{The Random Oracle Model}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-20}

\p{
    In the random oracle model every party — honest prover, verifier, adversary, reduction — gets
    oracle access to a function #{H} drawn uniformly from all functions of the appropriate type, and
    can learn its value only by querying it. The model is analysed by lazy sampling: the oracle
    keeps a table, answers a repeated query consistently, and answers a fresh one with independent
    uniform randomness. Security statements are then bounds in the number of queries #{Q} rather
    than in running time, which is why a random oracle proof yields a concrete number and a
    standard-model reduction usually does not.
}

\p{
    The reduction gets two powers from the model, and a security proof for Fiat–Shamir uses both.
    \strong{Observability}: the reduction sees every query the adversary makes, so it can read the
    prover's intended transcript out of the query log — this is what makes [straightline
    extraction](dhsorens-004J) possible at all, since the log substitutes for rewinding.
    \strong{Programmability}: the reduction chooses what the oracle answers, so long as it stays
    consistent — this is what lets a zero-knowledge simulator fix a challenge first and arrange for
    #{H} to return it. Neither power exists for a deployed hash. A verifier cannot watch a prover
    evaluate Keccak, and nobody can reprogram it.
}

\p{
    What the model discards is the code. A random oracle is an object no algorithm can describe more
    compactly than by its table; [Keccak](dhsorens-004F) is 24 rounds of a permutation, and
    [Poseidon](dhsorens-004H) is a few dozen arithmetic operations over the same field the protocol
    runs on. So a real prover can do things the model forbids by construction: evaluate #{H} inside
    a circuit, make #{H} the subject of the statement it is proving, or search for an input whose
    digest has a property it wants. The first two are non-black-box uses, and every known break of
    Fiat–Shamir in the standard model is one of them.
}

\p{
    Canetti, Goldreich, and Halevi showed in 1998 that the gap is real rather than an artefact of
    proof technique: there are schemes provably secure in the random oracle model and insecure under
    \em{every} concrete instantiation of the oracle. Their counterexamples were self-referential by
    design, and the field's working response for twenty-five years was that no protocol anyone would
    deploy looks like that. Khovratovich, Rothblum, and Soukhanov [removed that
    defence](dhsorens-0057) by exhibiting the same phenomenon for a standard
    [GKR](dhsorens-002Q)-based argument, and [the attacks](dhsorens-004X) are treated in detail
    separately. The model's failure mode is now attached to a protocol people ship.
}

\p{
    None of which makes the model dispensable, because every concrete number in a hash-based system
    comes out of it. The [Merkle collision term](dhsorens-0031), the [grinding](dhsorens-003A)
    discount, the query budget #{t} that separates the two conventions for [quoting bits of
    security](dhsorens-004N) — all are random-oracle quantities, and the alternative is not a better
    bound but no bound. The [Gentry–Wichs](dhsorens-004W) barrier says something stronger: succinct
    arguments for #{\mathsf{NP}} cannot be proved sound from falsifiable assumptions by black-box
    reduction, so a succinct system is going to rest on an idealised oracle or a knowledge
    assumption, and the choice is which.
}

\p{
    The variants in use trade one idealisation for another. The ideal permutation model replaces the
    random function with a random permutation the adversary may query in both directions, which is
    what a [duplex sponge](dhsorens-004Z) is actually built on, and Chiesa and Orrù analyse
    Fiat–Shamir there directly — finding, usefully, that indifferentiability from a random oracle is
    the wrong bridge, since it does not carry the knowledge soundness or zero knowledge the argument
    needs. The algebraic group model constrains a pairing-based adversary to producing group
    elements as known linear combinations, which is how [Groth16](dhsorens-003E) and
    [Plonk](dhsorens-003F) get their proofs. The [quantum](dhsorens-0051) version allows
    superposition queries. Each is a model, and a claim of the form "secure in the ROM" should be
    read as naming which one.
}
